{"cuisine/culture-of-aruba-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-aruba-cuisine","n":"Aruban cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Aruba","intro":[["The culture of Aruba, encompassing its language, music, and cuisine, is diverse and has been influenced by both regional and foreign cultures. One significant foreign influence originates from the Iberian Peninsula, which had a significant impact on the island for approximately 137 years, starting c. 1500. These influences were characterized by a strong religious presence, missionary activities, and economic exploitation."],["Since 1636, during the Eighty Years' War, the Dutch played a crucial role in shaping Aruba's identity, driven by their exploratory and mercantile spirit, contributing to the island's modern character."],["The Aruban tourism industry dates to the 1930s, when the first commercial airline landed on Aruba and a guest house was established in Oranjestad. Starting in the early 1950s, with the rise of a new wave in the tourism industry and the opening of the first luxury resort, Aruba Caribbean Hotel, a national aspiration arose to become \"The little Miami of the Caribbean\". However, this focus on developing a small island tourism economy (SITES or SIDS) led to rapid and uncontrolled expansion of the tourism sector. As consequence, it brought about a range of socio-ecological challenges and disrupted the sociocultural fabric of the island, known internationally as \"One Happy Island\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-aruba-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Aruba#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"This Is the Way We Cook! Asina Nos Ta Cushina","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jewell Fenzi","note":"Recipes from home cooks of Aruba, Bonaire, and Curaçao.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f3/Weststraat_Dakkapel.JPG/500px-Weststraat_Dakkapel.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ricky23","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weststraat_Dakkapel.JPG"}},"cuisine/kurdish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kurdish-cuisine","n":"Kurdish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Iran","intro":[["Kurdish cuisine consists of a wide variety of foods prepared by the Kurdish people. There are culinary and cultural similarities of Kurds and their neighbours in Iran, Iraq, Syria, Greece, Armenia and Turkey."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kurdish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Nandên: Recipes from My Kurdish Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Pary Baban","note":"Kurdish home recipes by an Iraqi Kurdish-born, UK-based cook.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Nanden/Pary-Baban/9781788797467","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781788797467"},{"name":"My Life, My Food, My Kurdistan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Chiman Zebari","note":"Family recipes and memoir from Iraqi Kurdistan.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Kısır","dish/k-s-r"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/3/3a/Xwarin-shingal1.jpg","w":500,"h":745,"by":"Bablekan","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xwarin-shingal1.jpg"}},"ingredient/corn-starch":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/corn-starch","n":"Corn starch","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Corn starch or cornstarch (American English), cornflour (British English) is the starch powder derived from corn grain. The starch is obtained from the endosperm of the kernel."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-corn-starch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corn_starch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",[["Pandebono","dish/pandebono"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"]]],["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Bah-uân","dish/bah-uan"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Bici bici","dish/bici-bici"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Bamboo shoot","ingredient/bamboo-shoot"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Shiitake","ingredient/shiitake"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Adzuki bean","ingredient/adzuki-bean"],["Black turtle bean","ingredient/black-turtle-bean"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Mung bean","ingredient/mung-bean"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d5/Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg/500px-Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Picasa author kalaya","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg"}},"ingredient/brandy":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brandy","n":"Brandy","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Brandy is a liquor produced by distilling wine. Brandy generally contains 35–60% alcohol by volume and is typically consumed as an after-dinner digestif. Some brandies are aged in wooden casks. Others are coloured with caramel colouring to imitate the effect of ageing, and some are produced using a combination of ageing and colouring. Varieties of wine brandy can be found across the winemaking world. Among the most renowned are Cognac and Armagnac from southwestern France."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brandy"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brandy"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Lobster Thermidor","dish/lobster-thermidor"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Boerenjongens","dish/boerenjongens"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Lobster Thermidor","dish/lobster-thermidor"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Lobster Thermidor","dish/lobster-thermidor"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Lobster meat","ingredient/lobster-meat"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Sherry","ingredient/sherry"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9a/Cognac_glass.jpg/500px-Cognac_glass.jpg","w":500,"h":423,"by":"Didier Descouens","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cognac_glass.jpg"}},"ingredient/ham":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ham","n":"Ham","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 9 dishes across 11 cuisines","intro":[["Ham is pork from a leg cut that has been preserved by wet or dry curing, with or without smoking. As a processed meat, the term ham includes both whole cuts of meat and ones that have been mechanically formed."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ham"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":11,"groups":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Uitsmijter","dish/uitsmijter"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Garbure","dish/garbure"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cotoletta alla bolognese","dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese"]]],["Minnesotan cuisine","cuisine/minnesotan-cuisine",[["Minnesota sushi","dish/minnesota-sushi"]]],["Neapolitan cuisine","cuisine/neapolitan-cuisine",[["Crocchè","dish/crocche"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Garbure","dish/garbure"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Okroshka","dish/okroshka"]]],["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine",[["Crocchè","dish/crocche"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Jamón","dish/jamon"]]],["Uruguayan cuisine","cuisine/uruguayan-cuisine",[["Caruso sauce","dish/caruso-sauce"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"],["Brown sauce","ingredient/brown-sauce"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Shellfish","ingredient/shellfish"],["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Chipolata","ingredient/chipolata"],["Linguiça","ingredient/linguica"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a9/Ham_%284%29.jpg/500px-Ham_%284%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Renee Comet (Photographer)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ham_(4).jpg"}},"ingredient/glutinous-barley":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/glutinous-barley","n":"Glutinous barley","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Barley (Hordeum vulgare), a member of the grass family, is a major cereal grain grown in temperate climates globally. One of the first cultivated grains, it was domesticated in the Fertile Crescent around 9000 BC, giving it nonshattering spikelets and making it much easier to harvest. Its use then spread throughout Eurasia by 2000 BC. Barley prefers relatively low temperatures and well-drained soil to grow. It is relatively tolerant of drought and soil salinity, but is less winter-hardy than wheat or rye. Wild barley may refer to H. vulgare ssp. spontaneum, but may also refer to other species in the genus Hordeum."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-glutinous-barley"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glutinous_barley"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Red bean paste","ingredient/red-bean-paste"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/20/Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg/500px-Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Cliff from Arlington, Virginia, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barley_(Hordeum_vulgare)_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg"}},"dish/grands-peres":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/grands-peres","n":"Grands-pères","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cuisine of Quebec","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Grand-pères, grand-pères au sirop d'érable or grand-pères dans le sirop d'érable is a traditional dumpling based dessert in Québécois and Acadian cuisine. The term pépère is also used to describe this dish in some regions of Quebec like Beauce. This dumpling is commonly served during \"le temps des sucres\" in sugar shacks."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-grands-peres"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grands-p%C3%A8res"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of Quebec","cuisine/cuisine-of-quebec"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/66/Grand-P%C3%A8re_au_sirop_d%27%C3%A9rable.JPG/500px-Grand-P%C3%A8re_au_sirop_d%27%C3%A9rable.JPG","w":500,"h":669,"by":"CoveyHill","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand-P%C3%A8re_au_sirop_d'%C3%A9rable.JPG"}},"ingredient/greenland-shark":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/greenland-shark","n":"Greenland shark","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The Greenland shark (Somniosus microcephalus), also known as the grey shark or gurry shark, is a large shark of the family Somniosidae (\"sleeper sharks\"), closely related to the Pacific and southern sleeper sharks. Inhabiting the North Atlantic and Arctic Oceans, they are notable for their exceptional longevity, although they are poorly studied because of the depth and remoteness of their natural habitat."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-greenland-shark"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland_shark"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Icelandic cuisine","cuisine/icelandic-cuisine",[["Hákarl","dish/hakarl"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Shark meat","ingredient/shark-meat"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/77/Somniosus_microcephalus_okeanos.jpg/500px-Somniosus_microcephalus_okeanos.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"NOAA Okeanos Explorer Program","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somniosus_microcephalus_okeanos.jpg"}},"dish/italian-beef":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/italian-beef","n":"Italian beef","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian-American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["An Italian beef is a sandwich of Chicago origin made from thin slices of roast beef simmered and served with a thin gravy on French bread. Common toppings are a choice between spicy giardiniera (called \"hot\") or mild bell peppers (called \"sweet\"). The entire sandwich may be dipped in the juice the meat is cooked in before serving with a side of French fries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-italian-beef"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_beef"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Au jus","ingredient/au-jus"],["Baguette","ingredient/baguette"],["Bread roll","ingredient/bread-roll"],["Giardiniera","ingredient/giardiniera"],["Roast beef","ingredient/roast-beef"]]},{"h":"More from Italian-American cuisine","links":[["Chicken Francese","dish/chicken-francese"],["Fettuccine Alfredo","dish/fettuccine-alfredo"],["Garlic bread","dish/garlic-bread"],["Italian tomato pie","dish/italian-tomato-pie"],["Rollatini","dish/rollatini"],["Spaghetti and meatballs","dish/spaghetti-and-meatballs"],["Stromboli (food)","dish/stromboli-food"],["Tetrazzini","dish/tetrazzini"],["Vodka sauce","dish/vodka-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3a/Mmm..._Italian_beef_%286286691399%29.jpg/500px-Mmm..._Italian_beef_%286286691399%29.jpg","w":500,"h":331,"by":"jeffreyw","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mmm..._Italian_beef_(6286691399).jpg"}},"dish/jingisukan":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jingisukan","n":"Jingisukan","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Jingisukan is a Japanese grilled mutton dish prepared on a convex metal skillet or other grill. It is often cooked alongside beansprouts, onions, mushrooms, and bell peppers, and served with a sauce based in either soy sauce or sake.  The dish is particularly popular on the northern island of Hokkaidō and in China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jingisukan"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jingisukan"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iek